{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"budget-reset-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Reset your budget from scratch","description":"Takes someone whose money feels out of control from real spending data to a working budget, automated payday set-up and a first-month review, pausing for approval between steps.","category":"budgeting","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build","review"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["dataset","text"],"output":["table","plan","checklist","report"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["financial"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["money-reset","spending-audit","budget-setup","monthly-review"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["categorize-expenses","build-monthly-budget","set-up-money-accounts-system","review-budget-vs-actual","fix-failing-budget"],"personas":["personal-finance-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"spending","description":"Two or three months of bank and card transactions (exported or pasted), or totals by category if that is all you have.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"money_picture","description":"Take-home income and pay dates, debts with rates and minimum payments, savings, and who the budget covers.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"country","description":"Country, so account types and free advice services can be framed correctly. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"spending-picture","file":"steps/01-spending-picture.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"budget-reset/01-spending-picture.md"},{"id":"new-budget","file":"steps/02-new-budget.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"budget-reset/02-new-budget.md"},{"id":"payday-setup","file":"steps/03-payday-setup.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"budget-reset/03-payday-setup.md"},{"id":"first-month-review","file":"steps/04-first-month-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"budget-reset/04-first-month-review.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Resets this household's budget the way a good money adviser would: start from what is really being spent, build a realistic budget from it, make it run automatically on payday, then check it against the first month and adjust. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse the approved figures.\n\n<spending>\n{{spending}}\n</spending>\n\n<money_picture>\n{{money_picture}}\n</money_picture>\n{{#country}}Country: {{country}}{{/country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only figures the person gave or confirmed. Mark estimates and missing numbers as [X] with a question.\n- Show the arithmetic so they can redo it.\n- No shame and no moralising about choices; the aim is a budget that fits the life being lived.\n- Do not name banks, apps or products; describe account types and features.\n- Never ask for account numbers or log-in details; tell them to remove these from pasted data.\n- If income does not cover essentials and minimum debt payments, say so in the step where it shows up and point to free, non-profit money or debt advice before continuing.","steps":[{"id":"spending-picture","file":"steps/01-spending-picture.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"budget-reset/01-spending-picture.md","text":"# Step 1: Spending picture\n\n1. Categorise the transactions into no more than about twelve categories. Exclude transfers between their own accounts and card repayments already counted as purchases.\n2. Average each category per month and convert annual or irregular costs (insurance, gifts, car) to a monthly figure.\n3. Compare average spending with take-home income and give the monthly surplus or gap.\n4. Flag leaks: subscriptions, fees and interest, duplicate charges, spikes. Ask about anything you cannot classify.\n\nSections: Spending by category, Irregular costs, Income versus spending, Leaks to check, Open questions. Stop for approval and answers."},{"id":"new-budget","file":"steps/02-new-budget.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"budget-reset/02-new-budget.md","text":"# Step 2: New budget\n\n1. Build a budget from the approved averages, not ideals: fixed costs, variable costs at realistic levels, a monthly amount for each irregular cost, a fun or personal line, debt payments and saving.\n2. Where it does not balance, show the trade-offs with their money effect and let the person choose; do not cut the fun line to zero.\n3. Set priorities in the usual order (starter buffer, expensive debt, emergency fund, goals), presented as a common approach.\n4. Keep to about eight to twelve lines.\n\nSections: The budget, Trade-offs chosen, Priorities, What changed from step 1. Stop for approval."},{"id":"payday-setup","file":"steps/03-payday-setup.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"budget-reset/03-payday-setup.md","text":"# Step 3: Payday set-up\n\n1. Propose three to five accounts or pots (bills, spending, buffer, goals) re-using what they have.\n2. List the payday transfers with amounts, showing they add up to take-home pay, and a bills buffer.\n3. Flag bills due just before payday and suggest asking to move them.\n4. Give an ordered set-up checklist, including the gap month while direct debits move.\n5. Define the weekly ten-minute check.\n\nSections: Accounts, Payday transfers, Due dates to move, Set-up checklist, Weekly check. Stop for approval."},{"id":"first-month-review","file":"steps/04-first-month-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"budget-reset/04-first-month-review.md","text":"# Step 4: First-month review\n\nAsk for the first month's actual spending by category (or transactions) if not yet given.\n\n1. Compare plan with actual per category and label each material gap one-off, timing or drift.\n2. Adjust drifting lines and move one-offs into sinking funds; keep the budget balanced.\n3. Check the set-up worked: transfers on time, no bounced bills, buffer intact.\n4. Set the routine going forward: weekly check, monthly review, a full reset each year.\n\nSections: Plan versus actual, What drove the gaps, Adjusted budget, Set-up check, Routine from here, Items for a professional."}]}
